Follow-up: Why separate this microservice?

Initially, reporting logic lived inside a larger reporting module.

Problems:

Large deployment size

Slow builds

Independent scaling impossible

One deployment affected all reporting APIs


So we extracted it into its own microservice.

Benefits:

Independent deployment

Independent scaling

Better ownership

Easier maintenance

Fault isolation



---

Why not keep it in the monolith?

Because:

Different scaling requirements

Separate release cycle

Different team ownership

Reporting consumed many external systems


Keeping it inside the monolith increased coupling.


---

Alternative designs rejected

Option 1

Cron jobs generating reports every hour.

Rejected because data became stale.


---

Option 2

Synchronous API calls to every downstream system.

Rejected because:

Slow response

Cascading failures

High latency



---

Final design

Event-driven updates using Kafka.

Dashboard reads pre-computed metrics.

Much faster.


---

Production issue

Kafka consumer lag increased after a large backfill.

Reason:

Consumer processed messages sequentially.

Solution:

Increased Kafka partitions

Added more consumer instances

Enabled batch consumption

Optimized DB writes


Lag reduced from nearly an hour to under five minutes.


